# The Hedphones I Despise
## Usability Journal Entry 1
About a year ago I purchased a pair of cheap wireless headphones. I bought them to use with Youtube while I do chores around my house, specifically while doing dishes. After a few months of usage I began to notice design issues that harmed the usability of the product. 
The case of the headphones is a sleek black plastic with no visible buttons. The affordance of this is a simple design that has no external controls. Despite this the back of the headphones have touch buttons that have various controls. There are no markings on the case that provide natural mapping of the prescence or function of the buttons. Furthermore because of their location and high sensitivity the buttons are very easy to accidentally press. Since I lost the user manual for them I do not know their exact usage but the buttons seem to control a few things. The buttons can pause the Youtube video, move ahead in a playlist, and activate Google's voice control feature on my phone. Since I do not have Google's voice control enabled on my phone pressing that specific button will open a pop up asking me to enable it which I can only shut down by going to my phone and doing so manually.
